; STORE-SHELL §5 check 2 (W3-12), guardedness leg — `r2-13`'s lines 2 to 4, corrected.
;
; `guardedB` is the second `WFS` conjunct and the second half of `Reachable.putS`'s
; premise. Unguarded recursion is the classic non-terminating spelling: a `mu` whose
; binder is reachable through positions that consume no value structure (the bare body,
; a union member, a refine subject). Each was admitted before W3-12 wired the check.
;
; Note the clause ORDER matters here and is asserted by the rejection strings: all three
; bodies DO mention their binder, so `usesBinderB` is satisfied and the spelling clause
; stays silent. `guarded` is the honest diagnosis, and it is the one the boundary gives.

; the bare unguarded body
(schema-put (mu "d" (var 0)))
(assert-code prev 1)

; the binder on a union spine: a union consumes no value structure
(schema-put (mu "d" (union anyOf (var 0) (prim int))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; the binder on a refine spine
(schema-put (mu "d" (refine (var 0) (filter "f" null true))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; guarded by an array: value structure is consumed on the way to the binder, so this is
; admitted — the clause discriminates
(schema-put (mu "d" (array (var 0))))
(assert-code prev 0)

; guarded by an object field, the ordinary recursive-record shape
(schema-put (mu "d" (object (f "next" opt (var 0)) (f "leaf" req (prim int)))))
(assert-code prev 0)

; two objects, not five.
(check)
